## Tool conventions

{% include 'tool-conventions.md' %}

## What to populate

- `sections` — per tool-conventions.md (typically a single
  numbered Steps section).
- `files` — `scripts/*` (executable + helpers) plus any
  `references/*.md`.
- `cli_surface` — optional. Populate when the skill ships a
  main shim.
- `rationale` — at most ~5 sentences or bullets: decisions
  made (script layout, single-vs-split helpers, error-handling
  shape) and alternatives rejected. Nothing else.

## Output construction

```bash
LOOM=~/.kiro/skills/home/loom/scripts/loom.sh
WD={{ run.workdir }}

$LOOM output init "$WD" --task design-author

$LOOM output add "$WD" --task design-author \
    --set frontmatter.name=demo \
    --set frontmatter.type=tool \
    --set 'frontmatter.description=...' \
    --set 'sections.0.heading=Steps' \
    --set 'sections.0.outline=...' \
    --set 'files.0.path=scripts/demo.sh' \
    --set 'files.0.purpose=Main shim.' \
    --set 'files.0.content_outline=...' \
    --set 'cli_surface.0.command=run' \
    --set 'cli_surface.0.args=<input>' \
    --set 'cli_surface.0.description=Execute the tool on <input>.' \
    --set rationale='Script layout and error-handling tradeoffs...'
```

Syntax: arrays use dotted numeric indices (`files.0.path`,
`files.1.path`); `.` nests. The writer validates the whole
document after every `output add`, so required sibling fields
(e.g. `path` + `purpose` + `content_outline` of one file) MUST
land in the same call.
